brigada init: set up example.invalid/imported

brigada runs coding tasks on the agent CLIs you are signed in to. A foreman
(an agent) turns your request into a plan of tasks; workers (other agents)
write the code in their own git worktrees; your check command tests it; and
an agent of another model family reviews it
when one is available (review.family=prefer; the same family or agent
otherwise). Four short steps follow.
The engine mirrors plan files (titles, task states, dependencies,
branches, open questions, extra repositories) to the brigada/plans
branch of your remote; turn that off with
`brigada project set plan_files=off`.
Nothing is written or started until you confirm at the end.

Signed in: claude, codex, opencode (2 providers, opencode providers list).
kimi: init can't check sign-in.
dsh (experimental): init can't check sign-in.

Step 1 of 4: the foreman
  The foreman plans the work and decides what happens when a task needs
  judgment. It runs whenever work finishes, so it uses that agent's quota.
  Only claude and codex can be the foreman.
    Which model should the foreman run?
      1  claude/sonnet               Sonnet 4 · Balanced
      2  claude/opus                 Opus 4 · Most capable
      3  claude/haiku                fastest; fine for small tasks
      4  claude/claude-sonnet-4[1m]  Sonnet, 1M context
      5  claude/claude-opus-4        Opus 4 · Most capable
      6  codex/gpt-6.1-sol           your codex default (~/.codex/config.toml)
      7  codex/gpt-5                 Earlier coding model.
      8  codex/gpt-5.4-mini          A smaller coding model for quick tasks,
                                     with a long description that must wrap
                                     within the menu without losing any of its
                                     words.
    Pick 1-8, or type harness/model [1]:
    How hard should the foreman think? Higher means better plans and decisions,
    and slower turns that use more of the plan's limits.
      1  medium  Claude Code's default
      2  low
      3  high
      4  xhigh
      5  max
    Pick 1-5 or type a level [1]:

  -> change later: brigada project foreman --harness H --model M --effort E, or
     run brigada init again

Step 2 of 4: workers
  Each agent CLI below can run tasks. brigada offers every model the CLI
  knows: for each task the foreman says how heavy a model it needs (light,
  standard, heavy) and how hard it should think, and brigada picks the
  model of that tier with the best record on this repo. Reviews prefer
  a different model family than the author's (review.family=prefer, the
  default); with one family they fall back to the same family or the same
  agent. Two families (e.g. claude + codex) give cross-family reviews.

  claude: signed in
    Use claude's models for tasks (sonnet and 4 more)? [Y/n]:

  codex: signed in
    Use codex's models for tasks (gpt-6.1-sol, gpt-5, gpt-5.4-mini)? [Y/n]:

  kimi: init can't check sign-in. If you're not signed in, run `kimi login`
  first, or leave kimi out.
    Use kimi? [y/N]:
    kimi has no sandbox: when it writes code it can run any command on this
    machine without asking. As a reviewer it only reads.
    Let kimi write code too, not only review? [y/N]:

  opencode: signed in to 2 providers (opencode providers list)
    Which models should opencode run? Its list spans providers, so pick the
    ones you pay for.
      1  zai-coding-plan/glm-5.3
      2  zai-coding-plan/glm-5.2
      3  opencode/deepseek-v3
      -  don't use opencode
    Pick numbers (1,3) or -, or type provider/model [-]:

  dsh (experimental): version 0.2.1-rc.2, sign-in can't be checked
  dsh needs DEEPSEEK_API_KEY exported before `brigada start`, or stored through
  dsh's own credential store.
    Use dsh? [y/N]:

  -> change later: run brigada init again
     your own defaults: ~/.codex/config.toml, ~/.kimi-code/config.toml

Step 3 of 4: tasks at once
  Each running task is one agent CLI working in its own copy of the repo,
  plus your check command, so this is bounded by your machine. This one
  has 14 cores. Each CLI's account also gets its own limit (claude 3,
  codex 2, kimi 1, opencode 2, dsh 1, brigada's defaults, never above
  this number); change any of them later with brigada config.
    Tasks at once [4]:

Step 4 of 4: the check
  A command that must succeed (exit 0) before a task's work is accepted.

  Found uv.lock, so the suggestion is:
    uv run pytest  pytest, from uv.lock
  Check command [uv run pytest]:

  -> change later: edit default_check in .brigada/project.yaml, then run
     brigada project import

